WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.440
How can you tell apart every cell in a tissue?

2
00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:08.100
Averaging a whole tissue hides its mix of cells, and the average shifts whenever

3
00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:12.380
that mix shifts. To see the mix, read one cell alone.

4
00:00:15.433 --> 00:00:19.073
Every cell in a tissue carries essentially the same genome.

5
00:00:20.333 --> 00:00:23.413
So the genome alone cannot separate neighbouring cells.

6
00:00:24.733 --> 00:00:28.773
What differs is which genes each cell actively transcribes into RNA.

7
00:00:29.853 --> 00:00:34.673
Single-cell RNA sequencing reads that active pattern cell by cell.

8
00:00:35.667 --> 00:00:39.967
First, enzymes usually separate the tissue into loose single cells.

9
00:00:41.327 --> 00:00:44.307
Each cell's captured RNA is copied into DNA.

10
00:00:45.807 --> 00:00:50.067
Every copy from one cell gets that cell's unique barcode, a short tag.

11
00:00:50.067 --> 00:00:55.467
With those tags attached, all the DNA is pooled and sequenced together.

12
00:00:56.567 --> 00:01:01.267
Next, software sorts the sequenced reads by barcode into a count matrix.

13
00:01:02.807 --> 00:01:06.927
Rows are genes, columns are cells, and entries count reads or molecules.

14
00:01:08.747 --> 00:01:10.947
Software then groups similar cells into a cluster.

15
00:01:10.947 --> 00:01:17.327
Comparing cluster genes to known profiles proposes an identity, called annotation.

16
00:01:18.567 --> 00:01:20.307
Look closely at those clusters.

17
00:01:22.107 --> 00:01:26.047
Software grouped the sampled cells by their RNA into separate groups.

18
00:01:27.347 --> 00:01:32.147
Predict: if a cluster has its own gene pattern, is it already a proven cell type?

19
00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:41.660
No: treat a cluster as a candidate type, not a measured function.

20
00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:47.200
Finer settings or deeper sequencing can split one cluster into two.

21
00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:53.600
Cells can enter and leave temporary states, so a cluster may be a state, not a type.

22
00:01:54.960 --> 00:02:00.020
Separating tissue also erases locations and probably depletes large, fragile

23
00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:01.020
neurons.

24
00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:04.770
Experimenters guard against those traps three ways.

25
00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:08.570
Quality control drops cells that fail checks.

26
00:02:10.010 --> 00:02:15.070
Spatial transcriptomics, like MERFISH or Slide-seq, reads RNA at known tissue

27
00:02:15.070 --> 00:02:20.730
positions. A candidate cluster earns a type claim when it recurs and location,

28
00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:23.150
shape, or response anchors it.

29
00:02:24.133 --> 00:02:28.133
Reading single cells unlocked biology tissue averages hide.

30
00:02:29.493 --> 00:02:34.513
In 2015, Hanchate and colleagues sequenced single mouse olfactory neurons.

31
00:02:35.893 --> 00:02:41.133
Many immature neurons expressed several receptor genes at low levels, while most

32
00:02:41.133 --> 00:02:44.393
mature ones carried one abundant receptor transcript.

33
00:02:45.517 --> 00:02:51.597
In 2022, Kamath and colleagues sequenced single nuclei from postmortem human

34
00:02:51.597 --> 00:02:54.337
substantia nigra, a midbrain nucleus.

35
00:02:55.597 --> 00:03:00.777
They sorted dopamine neurons into ten populations and mapped them with Slide-seq in

36
00:03:00.777 --> 00:03:07.937
macaque midbrain. One, marked by AGTR1, was highly susceptible to loss in Parkinson

37
00:03:07.937 --> 00:03:08.937
disease.

38
00:03:09.617 --> 00:03:15.597
In 2023, Yao, Zeng and colleagues profiled around seven million adult mouse brain

39
00:03:15.597 --> 00:03:22.957
cells. Roughly four million passed quality control and formed 5,322 clusters.

40
00:03:24.497 --> 00:03:29.577
MERFISH, in about 4.3 million cells from one mouse brain, placed them back in the

41
00:03:29.577 --> 00:03:33.717
anatomy. Still, not every cluster may be a true type.
